What is the output of the following code demonstrating dynamic method dispatch (method overriding)?

```java
class Animal {
    void sound() {
        System.out.print("Animal ");
    }
}
class Dog extends Animal {
    void sound() {
        System.out.print("Bark ");
    }
}
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Animal a = new Dog();
        a.sound();
    }
}
```

What will happen during compilation or execution of this casting code?

```java
class Dog {}
class Cat {}
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Cat c = new Cat();
        Dog d = (Dog) c;
    }
}
```

What happens when executing downcasting where the actual object in memory is NOT an instance of the target subclass?

```java
class Animal {}
class Cat extends Animal {}
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Animal a = new Animal();
        Cat c = (Cat) a;
    }
}
```

What is the output of this code utilizing Upcasting and method overriding?

```java
class Parent {
    void show() {
        System.out.print("Parent");
    }
}
class Child extends Parent {
    void show() {
        System.out.print("Child");
    }
}
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Parent p = new Child();
        p.show();
    }
}
```
